---
import DocShell from '../../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'doctor', label: 'Start with doctor' },
   { id: 'load', label: 'The page did not load' },
   { id: 'browser', label: 'No browser was found' },
   { id: 'session', label: 'No active session' },
   { id: 'voiceover', label: 'VoiceOver started unexpectedly' },
   { id: 'expect', label: 'A check fails that should pass' },
   { id: 'empty', label: 'The result is missing something' },
   { id: 'codes', label: 'Exit codes' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Troubleshooting"
   summary="<p>Fixes for the errors a1 prints most often. Each entry starts with the text of the message, so you can search this page for your error.</p>"
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section>
      <h2 id="doctor">Start with doctor</h2>
      <pre><code>a1 doctor</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>doctor</code> checks the browser and each screen reader. When something is
         missing, it prints the command that fixes it under <strong>Action items</strong>.
         <code>a1 setup</code> runs the setup steps for VoiceOver or NVDA.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="load">The page did not load</h2>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>✗ Error (internal-error): page.goto: net::ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED at http://localhost:3000/</code></pre>
      <p>
         Nothing is listening at that address. Start your development server, check the port,
         and run the command again.
      </p>
      <p>
         When a page loads slowly and the command times out, raise the limit with
         <code>--timeout &lt;ms&gt;</code>. The default is 10000. For a page that draws itself
         after loading, <code>--wait-for &lt;css&gt;</code> waits until an element appears.
      </p>
      <p>
         For a page behind a sign-in, <code>a1 check</code> and <code>a1 capture</code> take
         <code>--storage-state &lt;file&gt;</code>, a saved Playwright sign-in state.
         <code>a1 axe</code> takes <code>--cookie</code> and <code>--header</code>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="browser">No browser was found</h2>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>✗ Error (browser-unavailable): No usable Chromium-family browser is available for Playwright automation.</code></pre>
      <p>
         Scans, the accessibility tree, and the simulated screen reader need Chrome, Edge,
         Brave, or Chromium. a1 uses one that is installed on the system. On a machine with
         none, such as a CI image, install one:
      </p>
      <pre><code>npx playwright install chromium</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="session">No active session</h2>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>✗ Error (missing-session): No active screen reader session. Start one with "a1 sr start".</code></pre>
      <p>
         Most <code>a1 sr</code> commands act on a running session. Start one first.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr start --sr virtual http://localhost:3000/</code></pre>
      <p>
         A session ends after 30 idle minutes, so this can appear when you come
         back from a break.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="voiceover">VoiceOver started unexpectedly</h2>
      <p>
         Without <code>--sr</code>, <code>sr start</code> and <code>sr walk</code> use
         VoiceOver on a Mac that is set up for it. <code>sr press</code>,
         <code>sr type</code>, and <code>sr do</code> do the same when no session is active.
         Stop it, then say which screen reader you want.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr stop
a1 sr walk http://localhost:3000/ --sr virtual</code></pre>
      <p>Put <code>--sr virtual</code> in every script and CI step.</p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="expect">A check fails that should pass</h2>
      <p>
         <code>sr expect</code>, <code>sr goto</code>, <code>sr find</code>, and
         <code>sr wait --for</code> exit 4 when nothing matches. To see what the screen
         reader said, print the transcript.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr transcript --tail 10</code></pre>
      <dl class="defs defs--wide">
         <dt>The words are in a different order</dt>
         <dd>The simulated screen reader says the role first: <code>button, Pay</code>. Match a shorter string, such as <code>Pay</code>.</dd>
         <dt><code>expect --since</code> checked 0 phrases</dt>
         <dd>Either the checkpoint was set after the action, or the action announced nothing. Set the checkpoint before the action. If it still checks 0 phrases, the silence is the defect.</dd>
         <dt><code>wait --for</code> never matches</dt>
         <dd><code>wait</code> matches only announcements made after it starts. For one that already happened, use <code>expect --since</code>.</dd>
         <dt>The announcement arrives late</dt>
         <dd>Use <code>sr wait --for &lt;text&gt; --timeout 10000</code> before the <code>expect</code>.</dd>
      </dl>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="empty">The result is missing something</h2>
      <p>
         a1 tests the page as it is when the command runs. A dialog, a menu, or an error
         message that appears only after a click is not there yet.
      </p>
      <dl class="defs defs--wide">
         <dt>Content behind a click</dt>
         <dd><code>--click &lt;selector&gt;</code> on <code>check</code>, <code>axe</code>, and <code>tree</code> clicks one element before the test.</dd>
         <dt>Content that loads late</dt>
         <dd><code>--wait-for &lt;css&gt;</code> waits for an element before the test.</dd>
         <dt>Content behind several steps</dt>
         <dd>Start a session, get there with <code>a1 sr</code>, and read it from the session.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         After a page loads, a1 waits up to three seconds for animations to finish, so a page
         that fades in is tested as it ends up.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="codes">Exit codes</h2>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt>0</dt>
         <dd>The command ran and nothing it checked failed.</dd>
         <dt>1</dt>
         <dd>The command line could not be read: an unknown command or option, or a missing argument.</dd>
         <dt>2</dt>
         <dd>The command was used wrongly: an option value that is not allowed, or no active session.</dd>
         <dt>3</dt>
         <dd>The machine is not ready: no browser, or a screen reader that is not set up.</dd>
         <dt>4</dt>
         <dd>A check failed: a violation, or an announcement that was not made.</dd>
         <dt>5</dt>
         <dd>Something else went wrong, such as a page that did not load.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         Recording has its own list. See
         <a href="/guides/recording#problems">record a screen reader session</a>.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
